<header>
<h1>An explicit rank-two entropy-minimizing Fano degeneration</h1>
<p class="entry-meta">By Alec Kriebel, independent researcher.<br>Published <time datetime="2026-10-16">16 October 2026</time> · Version 1.0.0 · <strong>Unrefereed preprint</strong></p>
<p><a href="rank-two-fano-note.pdf">Read the paper (PDF, 5 pages)</a> · <a href="rank-two-fano-verification.zip" download>Verification archive (ZIP)</a> · <a href="https://zenodo.org/records/23411227">Zenodo record</a></p>
<p><a href="#plain-language">Plain-English explanation</a> · <a href="#result">The precise result</a> · <a href="#paper">Read on this page</a> · <a href="#collaborate">Collaboration</a></p>
</header>
<hr>
<section id="abstract">
<h2>Abstract</h2>
__FULL_NATIVE_ABSTRACT__
</section>
<section id="plain-language">
<h2>The idea in plain English</h2>
<p>A Fano variety is a geometric space with positive anticanonical curvature in the algebraic sense. Canonical degeneration theory associates an optimal limiting space to it. The limiting space can have singularities even when the original space is smooth.</p>
<p>A valuation measures the orders of vanishing and poles of rational functions. A divisorial valuation makes this measurement along one prime divisor, multiplied by a positive real scale. This example needs two weights whose ratio is irrational: no single divisor, at any real scale, gives the same valuation.</p>
<p>The construction combines an established three-dimensional example of Miao–Wang with the first Hirzebruch surface to obtain a smooth five-dimensional Fano variety. Exact equations select the two weights. The result identifies its unique entropy-minimizing valuation among all valuations and shows that the canonical first limiting variety is singular.</p>
</section>
<section id="result">
<h2>The precise result</h2>
<p>Work over the complex numbers. In projective four-space with coordinates [u:x₀:x₁:x₂:x₃], let Q be the smooth quadric u² + x₀² + x₁² + x₂² + x₃² = 0. Let H = Q ∩ {u = 0}, and let C be the smooth elliptic curve in H cut out by x₀² + 2x₁² + 3x₂² + 4x₃² = 0. Set V = Bl<sub>C</sub>Q, let E be the strict transform of H, and put X = V × F₁.</p>
<p>The first Hirzebruch surface F₁ has anticanonical polygon P = {(x,y): −1 ≤ y ≤ 1, −1 ≤ x ≤ y + 1}. Let D be its toric divisor with inward primitive normal (0,1). There are unique positive numbers a and b satisfying</p>
<p class="equation">∫<sub>−1</sub><sup>0</sup> s(2+s)²e<sup>−as</sup> ds + ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>2</sup> s(2−s)²e<sup>−as</sup> ds = 0,<br>∫<sub>−1</sub><sup>1</sup> y(2+y)e<sup>−by</sup> dy = 0.</p>
<p>The quasi-monomial valuation v with weights (a,b) along the transverse divisors E × F₁ and V × D is the <strong>unique global minimizer of the Han–Li H-functional among all valuations on X</strong>. The convention for finite-discrepancy valuations is H(v) = A<sub>X</sub>(v) + log ∫ e<sup>−λ</sup> dμ<sub>v</sub>(λ), with the probability-normalized asymptotic Duistermaat–Heckman measure of the raw anticanonical valuation filtration. The valuation's scale is part of the claim.</p>
<p>Its value group is exactly aℤ + bℤ. The paper proves that a/b is irrational, so the value group has <strong>rational rank two</strong> and v is nondivisorial. As an ordered subgroup of the real numbers, the valuation has <strong>ordered rank one</strong>.</p>
<p>The canonical first central variety is Z × F₁, where Z = Bl<sub>C</sub>Q₀ and Q₀ = {x₀² + x₁² + x₂² + x₃² = 0} ⊂ P⁴. The quadric cone's vertex o = [1:0:0:0:0] lies outside C, so Z retains an ordinary three-dimensional double point. Thus the central variety has singular locus {o} × F₁ and cannot be isomorphic to the smooth X. This first central pair is already weighted K-polystable; the second, polystable step changes no underlying variety.</p>
</section>
<section id="proof">
<h2>Why the construction works</h2>
<p>Strict monotonicity of the two moment functions gives their unique positive roots. A classical Hermite–Lindemann argument proves that b is transcendental. For any hypothetical positive rational ratio a/b = p/q, a universal resultant calculation would produce a nonzero rational polynomial vanishing at b. This proves irrationality for every rational ratio, rather than relying on numerical brackets or finitely many computations.</p>
<p>An adapted tensor-product basis identifies the associated graded anticanonical ring with the equal-degree Segre product gr<sub>ord E</sub>R(V) # R(F₁). Its Proj is Z × F₁. The raw section weight ak + b(j+m) has discrepancy correction m(a+b), leaving the canonical weight a(k−m) + bj. This checks the parameter and sign needed by the imported optimality theorem.</p>
<p>Miao–Wang's weighted-polystable degeneration and Wang–Zhu's toric soliton theorem provide the two factor solitons. The paper checks their weak product equation, using the bounded-potential results of Han–Li and the soliton correspondence of Blum–Liu–Xu–Zhuang. Han–Li's global criterion then compares v with <em>all</em> valuation competitors; Blum–Liu–Xu–Zhuang's absolute uniqueness theorem identifies the valuation and its canonical first center.</p>
<p>Convexity of the product moment functional alone establishes optimality only within the displayed two-parameter family. The established global degeneration and soliton theorems supply the stronger all-valuations conclusion.</p>
</section>
<section id="background">
<h2>Established inputs and prior work</h2>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.13999v2">Miao–Wang, <i>Optimal Degenerations of K-unstable Fano threefolds</i></a>: the quadric-blowup threefold and its weighted-polystable degeneration are established inputs, cited at §3.1 and Theorems 3.1–3.2. Their first preprint was posted in January 2024.</li>
<li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aim.2003.09.009">Wang–Zhu, <i>Kähler–Ricci solitons on toric manifolds with positive first Chern class</i></a>: provides the toric factor soliton.</li>
<li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.01010v1">Han–Li, <i>Algebraic uniqueness of Kähler–Ricci flow limits and optimal degenerations of Fano varieties</i></a>: Theorems 5.3 and 1.5 supply global optimality and comparison of infima. Their <a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/cpa.22053">generalized soliton work</a> provides the bounded-potential analytic input; the paper's Corollary 31 and Proposition 32 refer to the inspected author manuscript.</li>
<li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1017/fmp.2023.5">Blum–Liu–Xu–Zhuang, <i>The existence of the Kähler–Ricci soliton degeneration</i></a>: Theorems 1.2 and 1.3 supply absolute uniqueness and the soliton–polystability correspondence, using the numbering of arXiv:2103.15278v2.</li>
<li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s42543-022-00054-0">Li–Li, <i>Equivariant ℝ-test configurations and semistable limits of ℚ-Fano group compactifications</i></a>: earlier examples already have nontrivial canonical first centers. The paper does not certify the rational rank of their second example.</li>
<li><a href="https://doi.org/10.4171/OWR/2022/17">Blum's discussion in Oberwolfach Report 17/2022, pp. 888–889</a> describes the construction request addressed here, without a dimension bound or a product exclusion.</li>
</ul>
<p>This is an explicit five-dimensional construction using those established inputs. No historical firstness, exclusive priority, new general optimal-degeneration theorem, or three-dimensional nondivisorial example is claimed.</p>
</section>
<section id="status">
<h2>Status and AI use</h2>
<p>This is an unrefereed preprint developed, checked and prepared extensively with AI tools. The reported adversarial reviews were performed by AI agents. Independent human peer review and formal verification have not been completed.</p>
</section>
